{ Hi everyone! In this study, we're going to look at a strong defense for black - the Stonewall Dutch Defense. The Stonewall Dutch is somewhat popular among amateur players, and is a good choice for anyone, of any experience level. Magnus Carlsen has even played it!

So, how do we play the Stonewall Dutch? Let's find out! }
1. d4 { To reach a Dutch Defense, we can play 1. ... e6 or 1. ... f5. 1. ... e6 allows 2. e4 when we end up playing a French Defense. This is a better move order because it avoids a few annoying sidelines, and if you play the French I recommend this move. But if you don't, to be guaranteed to play a Dutch Defense, you should play 1. ... f5 (you can play it on the board.) } { [%csl Gf5,Be6][%cal Gf7f5,Be7e6] } 1... f5 2. g3 { This is usually considered white's strongest move. Now, we play 2. ... Nf6. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 2... Nf6 3. Bg2 { Next, we play 3. ... e6. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 3... e6 4. Nf3 { The key move of the Stonewall is 4. ... d5. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 4... d5 5. c4 { And now we play 5. ... c6. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 5... c6 { Black's position is called the Stonewall Dutch. Black can play this setup against almost all of white's moves. These moves may seem strange, but we'll see that they are, in fact, very strong. Let's explore this fascinating opening! } *

