[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ To truly understand the Stonewall, we first need to understand the pawn structure. The above position is actually impossible, but either way, we shouldn't play like it. We should develop our pieces before going straight for a Stonewall pawn structure. }
1. Bg2 { When you first see a Stonewall pawn structure, you may remember the advice "don't put all your pawns on one color!" But, the Stonewall structure is perfectly fine because, though black does make the dark squares weak, black controls the key e4-square.
Play 1. ... Nf6 on the board. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 1... Nf6 2. Nf3 { e2-e4 is a critical move for white in many 1. d4 openings, either because it gains space, or opens lines for attack. In the Stonewall Dutch, we not only prevent e2-e4, but we may even bring a knight to the e4-square!
Play 2. ... Bd6 to continue. } { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gf8d6] } 2... Bd6 3. O-O { Though we gain the e4-square, we have given up control of e5. But can't we just play ... Nbd7 if white ever occupies that square? Yes! White will have to be very patient if they want to take advantage of the e5-square. So, they usually start attacking on the queenside, to at least have something of a plan.
Play 0-0 on the board. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 3... O-O 4. Qc2 { As black, we have 3 main plans in the Stonewall. We'll study them each in the next chapters. } *

[Event "Stonewall Dutch Defense: Plan #1: King Hunt"]
[Date "2021.11.04"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "A90"]
[Opening "Dutch Defense: Stonewall Variation, Modern Variation"]
[StudyName "Stonewall Dutch Defense"]
[ChapterName "Plan #1: King Hunt"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6bIsRyMD/WfW0Czo7"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.04"]
[UTCTime "00:20:15"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Our first plan is the oldest, but also the most straightforward and probably the most fun: we hunt the white king! }
1. d4 { Let's play a Stonewall Dutch... } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf7f5] } 1... f5 2. g3 { 2. ... Nf6 is the move here. } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 2... Nf6 3. Bg2 { And now 3. ... e6. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 3... e6 4. Nf3 { Now comes the key Stonewall move. Do you remember what it is? } 4... d5 5. c4 { Now, we complete the Stonewall with 5. ... c6. } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 5... c6 { Our pawns are like a stone wall. They are sturdy and strong, but maybe not so flexible. } 6. O-O { In the Stonewall, our bishop wants to go to d6. So, what do you think we should do? } 6... Bd6 { It's important to know that we can only use this, our first plan, if white DOES NOT play 7. b3. Why? Because 7. b3 aims to play 8. Ba3, and in the Stonewall, an exchange of dark-squared bishops is good for white because then white gets more control over the weak dark-squares. To prevent this, we usually play 7. ... Qe7. But in Plan #1 our queen wants to go to e8 and then h5, which 7. ... Qe7 at least slows down. But, let's say white plays 7. Nc3. } 7. Nc3 { I know I just said white wants to trade dark-squared bishops, so why not 7. Bf4? Well, we don't mind 7. ... Bxf4 8. gxf4 because the doubled f-pawns make our attack even more promising.
Our main moves for Plan #1 are ... Qe8-h5, ... Ne4, ... Rf6-h6, and ... g5. Meanwhile, white will try to build up some queenside domination and dark-square control.
This plan for black is good, but won't always work. What are some other plans? We'll find out in the next chapters! } { [%csl Rh6,Bh5,Ge4,Yg5][%cal Rh8f8,Rf8f6,Rf6h6,Bd8e8,Be8h5,Gf6e4,Yg7g5] } *

[Event "Stonewall Dutch Defense: Plan #2: Bishop Maneuver"]
[Date "2021.11.04"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "A90"]
[Opening "Dutch Defense: Stonewall Variation, Modern Variation"]
[StudyName "Stonewall Dutch Defense"]
[ChapterName "Plan #2: Bishop Maneuver"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6bIsRyMD/qnRr6KBt"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.04"]
[UTCTime "00:45:19"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ Our second plan is related to Plan #1. We try to exchange our light-squared bishop for white's f3-knight before we launch our attack. Unfortunately, it's also a bit weak against that annoying 7. b3, it's still a very good choice, even against 7. b3. }
1. d4 { You know what to do! } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gf7f5] } 1... f5 2. g3 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 2... Nf6 3. Bg2 { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 3... e6 4. c4 { If white plays c2-c4 before we play ... d5, we should play ... c6 and only then ... d5. So, what does that mean we do here? } 4... c6 5. Nf3 { And let's build the Stonewall. } 5... d5 6. O-O { Where does our bishop want to go? } 6... Bd6 7. Qc2 { Phew! White doesn't play 7. b3, so we can play our main form of our second plan! (An adapted form after 7. b3 is in the next chapter.) But after 7. Qc2 or anything other than 7. b3, first, we castle. } { [%csl Gg8][%cal Ge8g8] } 7... O-O 8. Nc3 { Next, our light-squared bishop heads out on a journey! First stop: d7. } 8... Bd7 9. Rb1 { White's going to attack on the queenside. But we don't care about that! Our bishop just wants to continue its adventure. It decides to visit e8. } 9... Be8 { Our bishop may rest here for a bit, while we try to defend a bit on the queenside. We don't want to, but otherwise white may get an advantage. One important principal in closed positions is, unless there's some attack on a king, to be patient. We can just stop our opponent's threats for now, and eventually, our bishop will complete its journey - it'll go to h5 and trade itself for the knight on f3. We get rid of our bad bishop for a strong enemy knight.
So far, we've seen 2 plans that are good, but don't work as easily if white plays 7. b3. So, what plan do we have against 7. b3? Plan #3, of course! } *

[Event "Stonewall Dutch Defense: Plan #3: Queenside Counterpunch"]
[Date "2021.11.04"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "A90"]
[Opening "Dutch Defense: Stonewall Variation, Modern Variation"]
[StudyName "Stonewall Dutch Defense"]
[ChapterName "Plan #3: Queenside Counterpunch"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6bIsRyMD/LWtAW5vn"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[UTCDate "2021.11.04"]
[UTCTime "01:06:25"]
[Orientation "black"]
[ChapterMode "gamebook"]

{ At last, we've come to the modern main line of the Stonewall Dutch, when white plays 7. b3. Let's dive right in! }
1. d4 { I'm going do give you a test. Do you remember the first moves of the Stonewall Dutch? } 1... f5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 e6 4. c4 c6 (4... d5 { Remember, we play a different move if white's already played c2-c4. }) 5. Nf3 d5 { Nice job! } 6. O-O Bd6 7. b3 { White wants to play 8. Ba3 (after 7. ... 0-0 for example) and trade dark-squared bishops. Play 7. ... Qe7 to stop this. } { [%csl Ge7,Rg8][%cal Gd8e7,Re8g8] } 7... Qe7 8. Bb2 { Now it's OK to castle. } 8... O-O 9. Nbd2 { The key move of Plan #3 is to fianchetto our light-squared bishop with ... b6 and ... Bb7. Now is the perfect moment to start this plan! } 9... b6 { 9. ... Bd7, aiming for our second plan, is good too. But we're going to play our third plan! } 10. Ne5 { We have no reason to slow down. We just stick to our plan. } 10... Bb7 { Let the maneuvering begin! Unlike it the previous two plans, black won't sit passively on the queenside while white attacks there, instead, we push for active counterplay with ... Nbd7 and ...c5! This makes it especially hard for white to find many clear weak points. Very nice for black! } *

[Event "It"]
[Site "Teplits/Shoenau (Germany)"]
[Date "1922.??.??"]
[White "Geza Maroczy"]
[Black "Savielly Tartakower"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "A84"]
[Opening "Dutch Defense: Rubinstein Variation"]
[StudyName "Stonewall Dutch Defense"]
[ChapterName "Example Game"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6bIsRyMD/LC45B4BJ"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/caelT8"]
[Orientation "black"]

{ Every Stonewall Dutch player has to know this brilliant game by the great Savielly Tartakower, who employs our first plan to destroy Maroczy's king. I've annotated some of the opening moves relevant to the Stonewall, other than that, just sit back, relax, and enjoy. }
1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 { An alternate move order. } 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. a3 { Not the best setup. g2-g3 and Bg2 is better. } 4... Be7 { Bringing the bishop to e7 isn't best if we want to play a Stonewall. } 5. e3 O-O 6. Bd3 d5 7. Nf3 c6 { We have a Stonewall, but what's that bishop doing on e7? } 8. O-O Ne4 { ... Ne4 usually means we're going to play plan #1. } 9. Qc2 Bd6 { A waste of time. If we didn't play ... Be7 first, this would have taken one move, but instead it takes two. } 10. b3 Nd7 11. Bb2 Rf6 { Plan #1 it is! } 12. Rfe1 Rh6 13. g3 Qf6 { I prefer ... Qe8-h5, but Tartakower's move isn't bad either. } 14. Bf1 g5 { Maroczy's king must be feeling a little worried... } 15. Rad1 g4 16. Nxe4 fxe4 17. Nd2 Rxh2!! { Amazing. I am in awe every time I see this move. } 18. Kxh2 { And Tartakower wraps things up with... } 18... Qxf2+ 19. Kh1 Nf6 20. Re2 Qxg3 21. Nb1 Nh5 22. Qd2 Bd7 23. Rf2 Qh4+ 24. Kg1 Bg3 25. Bc3 Bxf2+ 26. Qxf2 g3 27. Qg2 Rf8 28. Be1 Rxf1+ { Another rook sacrifice. } 29. Kxf1 e5 30. Kg1 Bg4 31. Bxg3 Nxg3 32. Re1 Nf5 33. Qf2 Qg5 34. dxe5 Bf3+ 35. Kf1 Ng3+ { 0-1 White resigns. } { "The judges awarded this game the third brilliancy prize, although the majority of them declared in peremptory fashion that such sacrifices are incalculable in all their ramifications in advance and that, in consequence, they deserve no encouragement." - Tartakower
This is the kind of game every Stonewall player dreams of playing. It's simply amazing. } 0-1

